# Trivy suppressions — deliberately empty.
#
# An entry here does not make a finding go away; it records that someone read
# the finding, understood what it costs, and decided to accept it anyway. The
# file exists to make that decision legible in review and in `git log`, not to
# quiet a scanner that has become inconvenient. Silencing a check without that
# reasoning written down turns `make scan` back into decoration.
#
# Every entry is therefore three lines: the justification, the expiry, and the
# ID. Both comment lines are required.
#
#   - Justification: why this specific finding is accepted here — the control
#     that compensates for it, or the reason the check does not apply to this
#     resource. "Noisy", "false positive" and "not now" are not justifications.
#   - Expiry: `expires: YYYY-MM-DD` when the acceptance is temporary — waiting
#     on an upstream fix, a migration, a provider release. `expires: never`
#     when it is permanent, which should be rare and should be argued for.
#   - ID: one AVD check ID per line, `AVD-AWS-0000` or the short `AWS-0000`.
#     Trivy prints the ID with every finding.
#
# For example:
#
#   # Access logs for this bucket go to CloudTrail data events instead, so
#   # per-bucket S3 server access logging would duplicate them at extra cost.
#   # expires: never
#   AVD-AWS-0089
#
# Prefer a narrower fix to an entry here. An `#trivy:ignore:AVD-AWS-0000`
# comment on the resource in the .tf file scopes the suppression to the one
# resource that needs it, instead of to the whole repository, and it sits
# where the next reader of that resource will see it.
#
# There are no entries today: this repository holds no Terraform yet, and the
# intent is that it never acquires one without an argument in the pull request
# that adds it.
